WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.452 --> 00:00:02.102
- It's not about the tabloids,

00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:05.414
it's about you, putting in the
work, practicing every day.

00:00:05.414 --> 00:00:06.572
I just felt like I was waiting,

00:00:06.572 --> 00:00:08.762
and waiting, and waiting for everybody.

00:00:08.762 --> 00:00:10.279
Waiting for a producer to work with me,

00:00:10.279 --> 00:00:11.907
waiting for a songwriter to work with me.

00:00:11.907 --> 00:00:13.269
And as soon as I graduated high school,

00:00:13.269 --> 00:00:14.257
I wanted to take the next step.

00:00:14.257 --> 00:00:15.929
You know, my music
reflects my personality,

00:00:15.929 --> 00:00:18.177
and who I am, so if my music's unorthodox,

00:00:18.177 --> 00:00:19.697
maybe I'm unorthodox.

00:00:19.697 --> 00:00:20.901
There's cameras everywhere you go.

00:00:20.901 --> 00:00:23.198
There's one looking at me right
now, it's creeping me out.

00:00:23.198 --> 00:00:24.947
You know, these guys know
how to go up on stage

00:00:24.947 --> 00:00:29.073
with their band, and
control their environment.

00:00:29.073 --> 00:00:30.404
Rock the party.

00:00:30.404 --> 00:00:31.859
I want to make sure that
at the end of the day,

00:00:31.859 --> 00:00:33.129
we stand by our product.

00:00:33.129 --> 00:00:35.523
Everybody nowadays has an opinion.

00:00:35.523 --> 00:00:36.559
Everybody's a critic.

00:00:36.559 --> 00:00:39.741
Why we want to do music is
because that's what brings us joy.

00:00:39.741 --> 00:00:42.662
I was at the airport, and these

00:00:42.662 --> 00:00:46.803
huge Irish dudes start pacing around me.

00:00:46.803 --> 00:00:48.008
And I was like, alright,
it's going to go down

00:00:48.008 --> 00:00:49.508
at the airport, here we go.

00:00:49.508 --> 00:00:51.324
- He's an American singer, songwriter,

00:00:51.324 --> 00:00:53.118
producer, and choreographer.

00:00:53.118 --> 00:00:55.036
He's received many awards and nominations,

00:00:55.036 --> 00:00:57.059
including four Grammy Awards.

00:00:57.059 --> 00:01:00.269
He's sold over 100 million
singles and albums worldwide,

00:01:00.269 --> 00:01:01.997
making him one of the best selling

00:01:01.997 --> 00:01:03.842
recording artists of all time.

00:01:03.842 --> 00:01:08.009
He's Bruno Mars and here are
his top 10 rules for success.

00:01:10.193 --> 00:01:12.835
- Any young aspiring musicians out there,

00:01:12.835 --> 00:01:14.587
if music is what you want to do,

00:01:14.587 --> 00:01:16.781
if music is what you
love, and your passion.

00:01:16.781 --> 00:01:20.177
It doesn't take a fragrance,
it's not about the tabloids.

00:01:20.177 --> 00:01:23.730
It's about you, putting in the
work, practicing every day,

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:26.247
practicing your vocals,
practicing your instrument,

00:01:26.247 --> 00:01:28.673
practicing songwriting.

00:01:28.673 --> 00:01:31.462
And hopefully one day you
write the song, you know,

00:01:31.462 --> 00:01:33.601
the whole world wants to get down to.

00:01:33.601 --> 00:01:36.459
And I promise you, I
promise you, one day you're

00:01:36.459 --> 00:01:38.618
going to have your moment to shine,

00:01:38.618 --> 00:01:40.129
and you're going to have
a lot of people saying

00:01:40.129 --> 00:01:42.717
that you can't do it, and
you're not good enough.

00:01:42.717 --> 00:01:45.041
But I promise you, if you
go out there and you sing,

00:01:45.041 --> 00:01:47.198
and you put your heart and soul into it,

00:01:47.198 --> 00:01:50.034
and you follow your dream,
one day you're going to be

00:01:50.034 --> 00:01:51.805
sitting next to Ellen Degeneres.

00:01:51.805 --> 00:01:53.600
- (laughs)

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.111
(crowd cheers and claps)

00:01:55.111 --> 00:01:56.605
- You're going to be sitting
next to Ellen Degeneres,

00:01:56.605 --> 00:01:58.058
talking about how you broke records

00:01:58.058 --> 00:02:00.674
and rocked the Superbowl.

00:02:00.674 --> 00:02:01.507
Hit it!

00:02:02.698 --> 00:02:03.531
Stand up!

00:02:04.574 --> 00:02:05.991
Stand up America!

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:10.226
("Going to Fly Now")

00:02:11.652 --> 00:02:15.819
(crowd cheering and
clapping drowns out words)

00:02:20.772 --> 00:02:21.605
Yes, yes!

00:02:22.938 --> 00:02:26.855
(crowd screaming and cheering)

00:02:32.505 --> 00:02:33.847
I did it, Ellen, I did it!

00:02:33.847 --> 00:02:35.298
I did it!

00:02:35.298 --> 00:02:36.131
I did it!

00:02:44.795 --> 00:02:45.628
Let's go!

00:02:46.628 --> 00:02:49.378
(crowd cheering)

00:03:04.706 --> 00:03:06.145
I wanted to write a song because,

00:03:06.145 --> 00:03:10.228
I didn't want no one else
to write a song for me.

00:03:11.605 --> 00:03:12.654
And I didn't want to wait for anybody else

00:03:12.654 --> 00:03:14.036
to write a song for me.

00:03:14.036 --> 00:03:17.003
I think that's more of the
reason I was so frustrated.

00:03:17.003 --> 00:03:18.441
'Cause I got signed at a young age,

00:03:18.441 --> 00:03:20.832
and I just felt like I
was waiting, and waiting,

00:03:20.832 --> 00:03:22.306
and waiting for everybody.

00:03:22.306 --> 00:03:23.861
Waiting for a producer to work with me,

00:03:23.861 --> 00:03:25.765
waiting for a songwriter to work with me.

00:03:25.765 --> 00:03:27.278
You know, I play a little bit of guitar,

00:03:27.278 --> 00:03:28.511
I play a little bit of piano,

00:03:28.511 --> 00:03:31.497
so why wouldn't I just
want to do it myself?

00:03:31.497 --> 00:03:34.497
- After you moved to LA from Hawaii,

00:03:36.889 --> 00:03:38.975
you got there, and what was the reason

00:03:38.975 --> 00:03:41.898
to leave Hawaii in the first place?

00:03:41.898 --> 00:03:46.593
- I think I needed to take
a shot at the big time.

00:03:46.593 --> 00:03:50.760
I wanted to make my album,
start writing my own music.

00:03:52.787 --> 00:03:55.911
I've been performing my whole
life in Hawaii, in Waikiki,

00:03:55.911 --> 00:03:58.100
in parties, and weddings, and shows.

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:00.128
Anything you could think of.

00:04:00.128 --> 00:04:02.757
Restaurants, bars, and clubs.

00:04:02.757 --> 00:04:06.704
And I felt like I did a lot in Hawaii.

00:04:06.704 --> 00:04:08.208
As soon as I graduated high
school, I wanted to take

00:04:08.208 --> 00:04:12.041
the next step and hopefully
do it even bigger.

00:04:13.804 --> 00:04:15.504
- So you got there to LA and parked

00:04:15.504 --> 00:04:18.841
with just your suitcase, probably--

00:04:18.841 --> 00:04:21.002
- It wasn't even my suitcase.

00:04:21.002 --> 00:04:21.835
- (laughs) Any money?

00:04:21.835 --> 00:04:23.261
- Absolutely not.

00:04:23.261 --> 00:04:28.038
I probably had about $600
I saved up, 700 maybe.

00:04:28.038 --> 00:04:30.743
- Now what was the plan then, I mean,

00:04:30.743 --> 00:04:33.604
how did you feel at this special moment

00:04:33.604 --> 00:04:36.394
getting there and having no clue

00:04:36.394 --> 00:04:38.889
as to what's going to happen next?

00:04:38.889 --> 00:04:40.312
- It was exciting.

00:04:40.312 --> 00:04:42.308
I was young, my sister helped me out.

00:04:42.308 --> 00:04:44.659
She let me stay with her for a year,

00:04:44.659 --> 00:04:47.576
and I got signed to Motown Records.

00:04:48.751 --> 00:04:50.627
That didn't really work
out, but I had to learn.

00:04:50.627 --> 00:04:54.794
It's such a learning process,
and a lot of rejection.

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:58.242
I think people have to understand that

00:04:58.242 --> 00:05:00.325
that's just a part of the

00:05:02.069 --> 00:05:05.293
you know, what it takes to kind of finally

00:05:05.293 --> 00:05:06.695
come up with your own album,

00:05:06.695 --> 00:05:08.846
is that you need to be
rejected over and over

00:05:08.846 --> 00:05:11.625
and everyone telling you
you're not good enough.

00:05:11.625 --> 00:05:15.025
To kind of break through that,
and that's what it takes.

00:05:15.025 --> 00:05:19.192
You know before I got
signed, I used to go and play

00:05:20.447 --> 00:05:24.197
my songs for record
labels, and big executive

00:05:25.346 --> 00:05:26.954
presidents of labels.

00:05:26.954 --> 00:05:28.307
And that was always what they told me,

00:05:28.307 --> 00:05:32.053
that my music is too unorthodox,
too all over the place,

00:05:32.053 --> 00:05:34.588
and they don't know how to market that.

00:05:34.588 --> 00:05:37.279
And to me that was always
a hard pill to swallow,

00:05:37.279 --> 00:05:40.830
because my music reflects
my personality and who I am,

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:43.880
so if my music's unorthodox,
maybe I'm unorthodox.

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:48.217
Why should I change who I
am for business reasons.

00:05:48.217 --> 00:05:52.412
So it was more of a
statement of my freedom.

00:05:52.412 --> 00:05:54.455
I've always wanted to
be an artist, you know?

00:05:54.455 --> 00:05:56.718
And you think in your
mind, it's like, alright,

00:05:56.718 --> 00:05:59.252
I'm going to make an album,
then I'm going to do shows.

00:05:59.252 --> 00:06:01.228
I'm going to sleep all day,
wake up, do another show.

00:06:01.228 --> 00:06:03.100
Wake up in another city.

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:05.289
I'm finding out that it's not like that.

00:06:05.289 --> 00:06:08.110
I'm finding out that you got
to put in some hard work,

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:09.811
you know, for people to know who you are.

00:06:09.811 --> 00:06:11.551
There's cameras everywhere you go.

00:06:11.551 --> 00:06:13.986
There's one looking at me right
now, it's creeping me out.

00:06:13.986 --> 00:06:15.143
- [Interviewer] Oh yeah, always.

00:06:15.143 --> 00:06:16.257
It's my life, we've always

00:06:16.257 --> 00:06:18.127
had cameras.
- That's your life.

00:06:18.127 --> 00:06:19.255
It's all getting used to,

00:06:19.255 --> 00:06:23.424
but I'm living the dream
right now, the American dream.

00:06:23.424 --> 00:06:25.037
The reason why I like
Elvis is the same reason

00:06:25.037 --> 00:06:28.704
I like James Brown,
Michael Jackson, Prince.

00:06:29.582 --> 00:06:31.939
You know, these guys know
how to go up on stage

00:06:31.939 --> 00:06:36.003
with their band and
control their environment.

00:06:36.003 --> 00:06:37.447
Rock the party.

00:06:37.447 --> 00:06:39.333
No tricks, no smoke, no mirrors,

00:06:39.333 --> 00:06:42.000
just raw talent and great songs.

00:06:42.948 --> 00:06:44.409
- [Man] If you have to pick one,

00:06:44.409 --> 00:06:48.662
that you admire the
most, which would it be?

00:06:48.662 --> 00:06:50.383
- Who I admire the most?

00:06:50.383 --> 00:06:52.042
- [Man] James Brown, or--

00:06:52.042 --> 00:06:54.979
- I think as far as music's concerned,

00:06:54.979 --> 00:06:59.459
no one has done it in such a
big way than Michael Jackson.

00:06:59.459 --> 00:07:00.744
- [Man] You mean his own music, writing--

00:07:00.744 --> 00:07:04.641
- Just everything, all
around, the whole thing.

00:07:04.641 --> 00:07:06.714
When it comes down to music videos.

00:07:06.714 --> 00:07:09.382
The way he dressed, how innovative he was.

00:07:09.382 --> 00:07:12.454
I think you can go to
any country in the world,

00:07:12.454 --> 00:07:15.527
and five year old kids will
know who Michael Jackson is,

00:07:15.527 --> 00:07:18.694
because he's such a big part of music.

00:07:20.203 --> 00:07:24.099
His music just brought the
world together, basically.

00:07:24.099 --> 00:07:26.599
- [Man] And after that, Elvis?

00:07:29.132 --> 00:07:33.007
- Man, I've never came
up with a list before.

00:07:33.007 --> 00:07:35.942
I like these guys for
all different reasons,

00:07:35.942 --> 00:07:37.157
so it's hard for me to put them--

00:07:37.157 --> 00:07:40.181
- [Man] Just pick a few parts, and Elvis,

00:07:40.181 --> 00:07:43.113
what do you admire in him?

00:07:43.113 --> 00:07:44.135
- Like I said, the reason why

00:07:44.135 --> 00:07:46.718
I like Elvis so much is because

00:07:49.326 --> 00:07:52.909
if you know the story,
he's taking chances.

00:07:55.301 --> 00:07:58.534
He grew up listening to music that people

00:07:58.534 --> 00:08:00.951
weren't allowed to listen to.

00:08:02.499 --> 00:08:06.249
And I just like the fact
that he didn't care,

00:08:07.727 --> 00:08:09.233
and he did it.

00:08:09.233 --> 00:08:10.162
- [Man] Prince?

00:08:10.162 --> 00:08:11.484
- Prince.

00:08:11.484 --> 00:08:14.860
Being extremely innovative, and again,

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:17.777
someone that is such an individual.

00:08:19.126 --> 00:08:22.370
And a freak of talent,
you know, the guy plays

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:25.850
so many instruments, and
plays instruments, you know,

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:28.246
he could hang with the
best on any instrument.

00:08:28.246 --> 00:08:31.079
On the drums, on guitar, on piano,

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:34.766
and an incredible, incredible songwriter.

00:08:34.766 --> 00:08:36.159
- Do you have a hero?

00:08:36.159 --> 00:08:37.278
- Do I have a hero?

00:08:37.278 --> 00:08:38.528
- [Woman] Yeah.

00:08:39.379 --> 00:08:40.709
- Of course.

00:08:40.709 --> 00:08:41.542
- Who?

00:08:41.542 --> 00:08:42.490
- Superman.

00:08:42.490 --> 00:08:45.082
All we're trying to do,
every time we write a song,

00:08:45.082 --> 00:08:49.771
is to just feel an emotion
that we've never felt before,

00:08:49.771 --> 00:08:52.997
or, you know, be shocked
that wow, we did this.

00:08:52.997 --> 00:08:57.738
And if we don't feel that
way, then I don't think

00:08:57.738 --> 00:09:00.091
it's worthy enough to be on the album

00:09:00.091 --> 00:09:02.978
that we're trying to promote.

00:09:02.978 --> 00:09:04.460
And we want to make sure
that at the end of the day,

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:06.071
we stand by our product.

00:09:06.071 --> 00:09:07.603
Harshest criticism.

00:09:07.603 --> 00:09:10.650
Look, the thing is, you
know, in the beginning,

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:12.580
it's a little funky,
but you got to realize

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:15.319
that everybody nowadays has an opinion.

00:09:15.319 --> 00:09:16.944
Everybody's a critic.

00:09:16.944 --> 00:09:19.518
And you know, you could
watch my video on YouTube

00:09:19.518 --> 00:09:22.808
and leave a comment, you can
go to iTunes and buy my album

00:09:22.808 --> 00:09:24.475
and leave a comment.

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:27.827
You could tweet whatever
you want, you know.

00:09:27.827 --> 00:09:29.279
You're going to drive yourself crazy

00:09:29.279 --> 00:09:32.779
if you really take those things seriously.

00:09:35.265 --> 00:09:36.849
Not everyone's going to love ya.

00:09:36.849 --> 00:09:39.418
It got a little rocky

00:09:39.418 --> 00:09:40.589
two years ago.

00:09:40.589 --> 00:09:44.279
You know, that's when
we were at the bottom.

00:09:44.279 --> 00:09:46.241
And luckily I found Philip Lawrence,

00:09:46.241 --> 00:09:49.487
who's a tremendous person,
and a great songwriter.

00:09:49.487 --> 00:09:50.568
And me and him just clicked,

00:09:50.568 --> 00:09:53.735
and I found a friend that understands.

00:09:54.625 --> 00:09:56.280
We don't take it too seriously.

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:57.737
You know, we're happy doing music.

00:09:57.737 --> 00:09:58.920
That's why we want to do music,

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:02.003
is because that's what brings us joy.

00:10:03.755 --> 00:10:05.443
We were in the studio,
just writing and writing,

00:10:05.443 --> 00:10:09.541
hoping that this is going to pay
off, this is going to pay off.

00:10:09.541 --> 00:10:13.541
I was at the airport, and
these huge Irish dudes

00:10:15.056 --> 00:10:16.863
start pacing around me.

00:10:16.863 --> 00:10:18.379
And I was like, alright, it's
going to go down at the airport,

00:10:18.379 --> 00:10:19.673
here we go.

00:10:19.673 --> 00:10:21.335
And they go, "Ay!"

00:10:21.335 --> 00:10:22.762
I think they're Australian,
so let me change

00:10:22.762 --> 00:10:24.377
to Australian accent.

00:10:24.377 --> 00:10:26.562
He said, "Ay! Are you Bruno Mars?"

00:10:26.562 --> 00:10:27.997
And I was like, yes.

00:10:27.997 --> 00:10:30.139
(crowd laughs)

00:10:30.139 --> 00:10:32.064
They said, "Could you sign our forearms?"

00:10:32.064 --> 00:10:34.807
(crowd laughs)

00:10:34.807 --> 00:10:36.689
So they busted out a
Sharpie and I'm signing

00:10:36.689 --> 00:10:38.920
these like rugby players' forearms,

00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:40.258
and they're like, "Yo,
that Billionaire song,

00:10:40.258 --> 00:10:42.092
"you're a legend, you're a legend."

00:10:42.092 --> 00:10:45.205
- Oh that's so great, oh my God.

00:10:45.205 --> 00:10:47.495
Have you signed any other body parts?

00:10:47.495 --> 00:10:48.539
- (laughs) No, thank God.

00:10:48.539 --> 00:10:49.372
- Looking forward to that.

00:10:49.372 --> 00:10:50.468
- Yeah, you know when
you're a kid, you're like,

00:10:50.468 --> 00:10:52.331
oh, one day I'm going to
be signing all these girls,

00:10:52.331 --> 00:10:53.947
and then nope, not me.

00:10:53.947 --> 00:10:54.841
Big brutes.

00:10:54.841 --> 00:10:55.771
- Aussie rugby players.

00:10:55.771 --> 00:10:57.209
- Aussie rugby players.

00:10:57.209 --> 00:10:58.754
I try to be an artist.

00:10:58.754 --> 00:11:00.456
I moved up to California to be an artist.

00:11:00.456 --> 00:11:03.025
So I'm writing these songs,
and they're all from me.

00:11:03.025 --> 00:11:04.883
Never wanted to be a producer,
never thought about writing

00:11:04.883 --> 00:11:06.687
with other people, for other people.

00:11:06.687 --> 00:11:07.789
So I'm writing this song for me,

00:11:07.789 --> 00:11:08.910
and I got approached by a label

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:10.146
that heard one of these songs,

00:11:10.146 --> 00:11:13.611
and they said, look, we want
this song for another artist.

00:11:13.611 --> 00:11:16.318
We don't want to sign you,
we want to take your song

00:11:16.318 --> 00:11:18.337
and give it to another artist.

00:11:18.337 --> 00:11:21.231
At the time I was working
with Kara for the first time.

00:11:21.231 --> 00:11:24.923
And you know as a songwriter
she's written so many hits.

00:11:24.923 --> 00:11:26.117
And talked to her and I said,

00:11:26.117 --> 00:11:27.700
Kara, look, I don't know what to do.

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:30.834
I really want to be an artist.

00:11:30.834 --> 00:11:34.042
They want my music, this is
my music, this is my art.

00:11:34.042 --> 00:11:36.572
She looks at me and she
says, "Stop being a bitch."

00:11:36.572 --> 00:11:39.072
(crowd laughs)

00:11:39.072 --> 00:11:41.560
She says, "No one knows
who the hell you are.

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:43.177
"You have no story.

00:11:43.177 --> 00:11:45.468
"Sell your frickin' song,
what is it the last song

00:11:45.468 --> 00:11:47.659
"you're ever going to write, you bitch?"

00:11:47.659 --> 00:11:48.497
(crowd laughs)

00:11:48.497 --> 00:11:50.825
I was like, Jesus, Kara.

00:11:50.825 --> 00:11:51.658
- And so you did.

00:11:51.658 --> 00:11:54.260
- And I did, and that paid my rent.

00:11:54.260 --> 00:11:56.338
And that got me to stay up
here, and got me, you know.

